This has been done before, but doesn't work. First of all, all the 
randomly generated monsters are the wrong level. Second, everything 
_other_ than the level layouts and items on the floor will end up 
being different. In the worst case, one player will have a WoW 
dropped by a monster early on, and another player won't.

The main problem is that player actions can cause random numbers to 
be generated, which will cause later events to use a different part 
of the random number sequence. Essentially, any event which causes a 
random number to be generated causes the rest of the game to be 
different. Just seeding the random number generator the same way is 
not even close to adequate.

The solution is to modify the random number generator so, say, that 
rolling to-hit an extra time won't change the numbers used, say, to 
decide what effect you get from a fountain. To give a few more 
examples, the random number generator should have the following 
properties:

 - Dungeon levels should have the same layout and items across all 
   instances.
 - Monsters generated on a given level should be the same for any two 
   instances with the same character level, but may be different 
   otherwise. (Monster levels depend on the level of the visiting 
   player.)
 - If a monster drops a Wand of Wishing or other notable item in one
   game, that item shall be dropped in all games if similar 
   conditions occur.
 - If one player is killed on the first hit of a fight by deadly 
   poison, then all non-poison-resistant characters taking the same 
   hit should be killed in the same way.
 - Fountain quaffs, foocubus encounters, random polymorphs, etc. 
   should be the same if done in similar circumstances.
 - No one should leave or get bones. (It's awfully complicated 
   otherwise.)

This sounds like it'd require really invasive changes to NetHack, but 
it actually doesn't. Here's how I'd do it. Note that this is really 
math-y, so feel free to skip the next few paragraphs.

Let r(s) be a function which produces a random number from seed s 
  (that is, any sequential random number generator).
Let G(V) be a function which produces a random number from a tuple V.
Let R(V) be a parameterized random number generator, such that
  R(V)={
         if(q[V] is defined)
           let q[V] = r(q[V])
         else
           q[V] = G(V)
         return q[V]
       }
  (where q is a hash-map or similar)

For each call to the random number generator in the game, assign a 
unique number N. Let P[N] be any of {dlvl, clvl} relevant to the 
interpretation of the result of the call.

Now for any call to the random number generator, use R( {N,P[N]} )
as the new RNG. Two games with the same functions r(s) and G(V) will 
be as similar as is possible given that players may use different 
strategies.

I call this _duplicate NetHack_, as opposed to seeded NetHack, to 
reflect the fact that games should be as close to identical as is 
possible.

Ok, now the tricky bit is automating the process of numbering 
NetHack's 2000-some random number generator calls, and not making a 
mess in the process. Tricky, but not impossible. I may make the 
attempt; if it works, I'll post here.

